this is a production of Cornell  University  all right thank you Dave so yeah I'm  glad that Lynde I presented a few of the  details about the partial budget  analysis because I'm gonna follow up on  that a little bit that's what this  survey was in part about so quick  background story is that I started  working with Dave within New York State  soil health initiative last fall and  they've had this idea to expand upon the  idea of these use case studies and look  at a little bit of a broader picture of  the costs and the benefits that farmers  were experiencing across the state and  try to capture many different types of  cropping systems so Dave and I came up  with this questionnaire that we  distributed so farmers across the state  during the fall in the winter of last  year and overall we collected 182  responses covering almost all the  companies in New York and we broke those  down into when we asked farmers to  report the types of crops that they grew  some of the practices that they used  especially focusing on cover crops  reduced tillage and soil amendments  which included organic matter like newer  compost mulch woodchips things like that  so we got a pretty pretty good  representation across the state  especially capturing some of these more  active agricultural districts let's see  here in this slide and then one of the  more interesting things right up front  that came out of this was that we asked  first indicate some of the major  constraints that they we're seeing on  their farm and the top three that rose  to the top here were poor drainage soil  compaction and soil erosion and all  three of these are really problems that  can be addressed with reduced tillage  cover crops and bring em in amendments  um so this is a this slide is a  simplified version of the table that you  might see on the handout that you have  and what we did here is we asked first  when testify the pot or in this one on  the benefits of reduced village that  they were seeing and again going back to  Lynn's talk these could be increases in  income or reductions in expenses and  what really Kim is up here is for  reduced tillage less labour was a big  one  and another aspect of of this this  result here is that we separated out  farmers who were using reduced tillage  on exclusively vegetable crops and  exclusively corn and soy and we were  interested in in looking at those those  two groups and seeing if they were  seeing different benefits and indeed  there were there were a few differences  this next slide is a super illustration  of those differences you can see the  vegetables and the green there and the  corns play is yellow this is this is  showing that vegetable growers reported  that they're seeing greater yields from  the reduced tillage practices as a  higher rate than the quantity growers  and then of course in the left there you  can see overall rate at which producers  were in greater yields which is  significant so it's about 50% there  and we also looked the the difference of  crops again we separated that out  according the strugglers and the  vegetable growers here those two groups  and again we see that they have  different they're experiencing different  benefits and also it's don't really  noticeable that erosion the erosion  control benefit of the cover crops is  really noticeable over 95% of instantly  growers are reporting that that's really  number one for the cover crops and then  more illustrations here these the  differences between the two crop groups  again we see that the vegetable growers  then are the green there and they're  reporting almost an 80% rate greater  cash crop yields and they're using their  cover crops but we're not really seeing  that benefit quite as much in the corn  so many however once my growers do see  other benefits here we see that they're  sort of combining cover crops within our  last operations and I'm using those  cover crops as animal force and that's  if they're seeing but didn't visible  growers tend to have less integration I  think with with livestock and so they're  not seeing those benefits  and so David David Montgomery especially  just talked about how farmers can use  these practices to organic matter  over some minatom and that's that's  really important is trying to figure out  how long some of those changes might  take  so we these farmers that completed the  survey today on told us basically how  long they've been doing these practices  and we grouped them by their experience  level one to five years five to ten  years ten to twenty and twenty plus  years and what we saw were some pretty  interesting evidence that emerged from  this this slide is showing a percentage  of each group at practices reduced  tillage on the percentage of each group  reporting less erosion and we can see  here pretty clearly as time goes on on  your x-axis there you're pushing to make  plus years and the percent of farmers  reporting less erosion is almost at 100%  whereas if we start down here and we  look at the one to five years  twenty-five percent there so um two  things maybe to note here is that you do  see some benefits right away but don't  likelihood that you're going to see  those benefits increase over time is  evident and we use the chi-square test  there to show that that's a significant  association sinking here this is also a  really interesting result not necessary  necessarily related to sort of health  but we're seeing that more experienced  farmers who've been doing reduced  tillage in longer they're taking a  better advantage of these posture  programs so this could be a way to an  area of improvement in terms of policy  maybe outreach  trying to get some of these these  farmers who are getting started training  fit them enrolled in some of these some  of these programs  and finally we're seeing often yields at  about 10 years so once once these  farmers have been using cover crops for  10 years the percentage of them that  report 